852 0 obj
<</Title(The subsidy policy has three mutually exclusive consumption bands. A household consuming at most 100 units receives a full subsidy. A household consuming 101\205200 units receives a partial subsidy only when it has a ration card. Above 200 units, subsidy is available only when a household member is older than 80 years. Otherwise, no subsidy is given.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 792 0 R/First 862 0 R/Last 863 0 R/Count -13>>
endobj

853 0 obj
<</Title(1.2 Why separate if statements can produce multiple results)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 629 0 R/Prev 792 0 R/First 864 0 R/Last 864 0 R/Count -1/Next 793 0 R>>
endobj

854 0 obj
<</Title(For the eight test cases above, both implementations give the intended policy outcome. The separate-if version returns a list because it can collect multiple decisions; the chained version returns one decision string. To expose the risk of overlapping independent conditions, a poorly written implementation such as `if units <= 200` followed by `if units >= 100` would execute both blocks for values from 100 through 200.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 793 0 R>>
endobj

855 0 obj
<</Title(The for-loop version has a subtle issue if blank entries should not count: `range\(3\)` counts loop iterations, including blank entries. A robust version must use a while loop, or use a separate counted-attempt loop that repeats blank entries without consuming one of the three wrong attempts. For this requirement, the while loop directly models the number of counted attempts and is therefore the more natural design.)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 798 0 R>>
endobj

856 0 obj
<</Title(2.3 Changed condition: two extra attempts after security answer)/Dest[13 0 R/Fit]/Parent 7 0 R/Prev 798 0 R/First 865 0 R/Last 865 0 R/Count -1/Next 866 0 R>>
endobj

